                    "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
                    "body": "> I had some commands with fixed labels, so something like \"Visited paths (core)\" will be useful. Also, with gen_sort.z(), i can specify it to something like \"Visited path (z)\". Maybe add information like global/local search that is mentioned in docs\r\n> \r\n> It will be great not just to remember what i typed, but also to see it in the prompt\r\n\r\nI guess this makes sense, but I am really hesitant about adding this customization. It feels a bit too much (add new argument as `opts` does not fit, document, and test it) compared to the net gain.\r\n\r\nI'll think about it, but probably the suggested solution here would be to write own `vim.ui.select()` wrapper or use `select_{path,label}` functions as is.",
                    "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I had some commands with fixed labels, so something like \"Visited paths (core)\" will be useful. Also, with gen_sort.z(), i can specify it to something like \"Visited path (z)\". Maybe add information like global/local search that is mentioned in docs</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">It will be great not just to remember what i typed, but also to see it in the prompt</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I guess this makes sense, but I am really hesitant about adding this customization. It feels a bit too much (add new argument as <code class=\"notranslate\">opts</code> does not fit, document, and test it) compared to the net gain.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I'll think about it, but probably the suggested solution here would be to write own <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.ui.select()</code> wrapper or use <code class=\"notranslate\">select_{path,label}</code> functions as is.</p>",
                    "bodyText": "I had some commands with fixed labels, so something like \"Visited paths (core)\" will be useful. Also, with gen_sort.z(), i can specify it to something like \"Visited path (z)\". Maybe add information like global/local search that is mentioned in docs\nIt will be great not just to remember what i typed, but also to see it in the prompt\n\nI guess this makes sense, but I am really hesitant about adding this customization. It feels a bit too much (add new argument as opts does not fit, document, and test it) compared to the net gain.\nI'll think about it, but probably the suggested solution here would be to write own vim.ui.select() wrapper or use select_{path,label} functions as is.",
